        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "If by “best” you mean “currently most precise and broadly trusted,” then as of May 2026 there still isn’t a single universally accepted value of the Hubble constant \\(H_0\\). The field remains split between two extremely mature measurement programs:\n\n- “Early universe” inference (primarily CMB + ΛCDM): about 67–68 km/s/Mpc\n- “Late universe” direct measurements: about 72–73 km/s/Mpc\n\nThat split — the Hubble tension — is still unresolved and, if anything, has become harder to dismiss. citeturn0search9turn0search16\n\nThe current leading contenders are roughly:\n\n| Method | \\(H_0\\) (km/s/Mpc) | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| entity[\"organization\",\"Planck Collaboration\",\"ESA cosmology mission\"] CMB + ΛCDM | \\(67.4 \\pm 0.5\\) | Highest precision overall; model-dependent |\n| entity[\"organization\",\"SH0ES\",\"Supernova H0 for the Equation of State collaboration\"] Cepheid distance ladder | \\(73.0 \\pm 1.0\\) | Best-established local/direct ladder |\n| TRGB (Freedman group) | ~\\(69–70\\) | Intermediate value |\n| DESI inverse distance ladder / model-independent reconstructions | ~\\(68–69\\) | Increasingly influential |\n| GW “standard sirens” | still broad uncertainties | Promising but not yet competitive |\n\nThe key issue now is less “which measurement is technically best?” and more “which assumptions do you trust?”\n\nA useful way to frame the landscape in 2026:\n\n1. Best precision:\n   The entity[\"organization\",\"Planck Collaboration\",\"ESA cosmology mission\"] result still dominates statistically:\n   \\[\n   H_0 \\approx 67.4 \\pm 0.5\n   \\]\n   but only within ΛCDM cosmology. citeturn0search2turn0search14\n\n2. Best direct/local measurement:\n   entity[\"organization\",\"SH0ES\",\"Supernova H0 for the Equation of State collaboration\"] remains the benchmark:\n   \\[\n   H_0 \\approx 73\n   \\]\n   with JWST follow-up largely reinforcing rather than collapsing the result. citeturn0search0turn0search9\n\n3. Best “consensus-leaning compromise”:\n   A lot of newer DESI-based and model-independent reconstructions are clustering around:\n   \\[\n   H_0 \\sim 68.5 - 69.5\n   \\]\n   which sits between Planck and SH0ES. citeturn0academia24turn0academia23turn0search13\n\nSo if you forced cosmologists into categories:\n\n- “Most precise”: Planck\n- “Most direct”: SH0ES\n- “Most methodologically conservative / emerging synthesis”: DESI + inverse-distance-ladder style analyses\n- “Most likely to break the tie eventually”: gravitational-wave standard sirens\n\nAnd the important meta-point is that by 2026 the tension is no longer widely treated as “probably just one bad calibration.” Multiple independent methods now tend to reproduce each camp. citeturn0search9turn0search16turn0search11"
          ]
        },
